WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.920
If we keep ourselves positive, if we
enjoy the process, we will be learning.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:09.150
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann, and
today I want to talk about like,

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:11.220
how do we know we're learning?

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:12.240
Okay.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.510
Uh, remember, if you enjoy
my videos, please subscribe.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.340
Click on the bell for notifications.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.340
If you're following me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:26.490
So, very often when we're engaged
in some kind of language learning

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.540
activity, we don't know if we
are actually learning anything.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.620
It's not like if we lift weights,
uh, our muscles are tired.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:39.510
We have the sense that we have worked,
we've done some work, and we're fairly

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:43.500
confident that we're contributing
to a certain level of fitness once

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.145
we leave the gym or after working
out for half an hour, we do feel

00:00:47.145 --> 00:00:48.615
a certain tightness in our body.

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:51.585
We feel that we've done something and
language learning is not like that.

00:00:51.825 --> 00:00:55.175
So how do we know if we are
in fact achieving anything?

00:00:55.185 --> 00:00:59.205
If we're reading, we might
enjoy what we're reading.

00:00:59.205 --> 00:01:01.065
We might enjoy the subject matter.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:03.885
If it's a novel or a book on
history or whatever it might be,

00:01:03.885 --> 00:01:05.265
we might find that interesting.

00:01:05.475 --> 00:01:09.255
But how do we know that we are
actually improving in the language if

00:01:09.255 --> 00:01:10.605
that's what we would like to achieve?

00:01:11.205 --> 00:01:17.100
Or conversely, if we're doing grammar,
or reading a dictionary, like how do

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.560
we know that we are actually improving?

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.149
I have read grammar books.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:22.740
I have even leafed through dictionaries.

00:01:23.009 --> 00:01:26.580
I never know if I'm actually
retaining anything, if I'm

00:01:26.585 --> 00:01:27.990
actually learning anything.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:34.440
Similarly, when I'm reading or listening
or even saving words on LingQ, how

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:35.800
do I know that I'm learning anything?

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:36.660
I don't know.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:37.919
I don't know.

00:01:38.039 --> 00:01:43.545
So my advice here is, is several,
first of all: Try to do things that you

00:01:43.545 --> 00:01:48.885
enjoy doing so at the very least, even
if you don't have a sense that you're

00:01:48.885 --> 00:01:52.635
improving, at least you have the sense
that you are enjoying what you're doing.

00:01:52.815 --> 00:01:57.885
So I enjoy listening to my
whatever podcast it might

00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.865
be in Persian, for example.

00:02:00.135 --> 00:02:00.975
I enjoy doing that.

00:02:01.425 --> 00:02:07.515
I enjoy doing the sentence view of
difficult content and then doing the

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.745
matching pairs and the sort of scrambled
sentence that I have to put back together

00:02:11.745 --> 00:02:17.895
again, I alert you that this function
right now only exists in the iOS version.

00:02:18.495 --> 00:02:22.095
We're trying to perfect it there,
and then we will introduce it as

00:02:22.095 --> 00:02:26.445
well in Android and in the web
version, so please be patient.

00:02:26.864 --> 00:02:27.945
I enjoy doing that.

00:02:28.244 --> 00:02:32.864
It slows me down in terms of acquiring
new words, but it gives me a good

00:02:32.864 --> 00:02:35.114
grasp of how the language works.

00:02:35.204 --> 00:02:36.195
I enjoy doing that.

00:02:36.435 --> 00:02:41.714
I don't know for a fact that I'm better
off doing that than just reading maybe.

00:02:42.165 --> 00:02:43.394
And, and of course I vary it.

00:02:43.394 --> 00:02:47.954
I do some, sort of, just reading
through without picking through the

00:02:47.954 --> 00:02:52.155
sentence in such great detail, and
I vary it with this other activity.

00:02:53.054 --> 00:02:58.725
But I'm not sure which of these activities
is helping me the most, but I do know

00:02:58.725 --> 00:03:03.375
that whichever one I'm engaged in,
just reading or picking through the

00:03:03.765 --> 00:03:05.625
sentences, I know I enjoy doing it.

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:07.875
So I think that's a very important factor.

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:11.565
Of course, over time you will
see that you have improved.

00:03:11.745 --> 00:03:15.475
You will see that your word count
at LingQ has gone up, number

00:03:15.475 --> 00:03:17.505
of LingQs created has gone up.

00:03:17.775 --> 00:03:22.215
All of the statistics that we have
that measure your activity, these

00:03:22.215 --> 00:03:25.515
are good indicators that you are
progressing in the language, even

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:30.615
if you're not aware of that feeling,
that you know you're getting better.

00:03:30.705 --> 00:03:34.620
And I have said before that, In the
initial three to six months, we have

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.570
a sense of a steep progression as
we're learning high frequency words.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:43.290
A language which had no meaning to
us, all of a sudden has meaning.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:47.460
But then thereafter, there's this
long period, the plateau, the

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.910
doldrums, where we have no great
sense of progressing in the language.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:57.525
And so we wonder, is this activity
actually enabling me to, you know, learn.

00:03:57.525 --> 00:03:58.395
Am I learning?

00:03:58.815 --> 00:04:01.935
And I think there it's
important to trust the process.

00:04:02.265 --> 00:04:06.195
It's important to avoid doing
activities that you don't like doing.

00:04:06.195 --> 00:04:07.695
Like I don't do drills.

00:04:07.935 --> 00:04:11.325
I've seen suggestions that, for example,
after reading something, you should

00:04:11.535 --> 00:04:13.545
try and summarize it in your own words.

00:04:13.575 --> 00:04:14.595
I never do that.

00:04:14.775 --> 00:04:16.125
To me that's unnatural.

00:04:16.125 --> 00:04:17.774
I would rather just speak to someone.

00:04:17.894 --> 00:04:19.394
I sometimes speak with my tutor.

00:04:20.505 --> 00:04:25.275
Uh, some material that she has sent my
way, a podcast on, you know, Iranian

00:04:25.275 --> 00:04:26.955
identity or whatever it might be.

00:04:27.075 --> 00:04:30.375
I'd rather just naturally have
a conversation than try to sort

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:34.065
of artificially, uh, you know,
describe what it is I just read.

00:04:34.095 --> 00:04:35.505
So I don't do those things.

00:04:35.685 --> 00:04:37.395
I don't try to analyze what I'm doing.

00:04:37.395 --> 00:04:38.715
I avoid doing things that I don't like.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:43.100
But if you like doing those things,
by all means do them because it's so

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:47.870
hard in language learning to know at
any given point if in fact all of our

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:50.570
activities are contributing to learning.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.750
So we're best off to do
things that we enjoy doing.

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:57.980
If we enjoy those things and
if we continue being active in

00:04:57.980 --> 00:04:59.990
the language, we will learn.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:01.160
We will learn.

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:06.795
And, and I go back to my famous, not my
famous, but the what this one director of

00:05:06.795 --> 00:05:10.785
the language department at San Diego State
University once told me there's only three

00:05:10.785 --> 00:05:14.745
things that matter in language learning:
the learner's attitude, time on...

00:05:14.955 --> 00:05:18.375
time with the language
and the ability to notice.

00:05:18.435 --> 00:05:23.055
But to me, the ability to notice
comes from a positive attitude,

00:05:23.115 --> 00:05:26.465
a desire to notice, and spending
a lot of time with the language.

00:05:27.015 --> 00:05:31.635
If we keep ourselves positive, if we
enjoy the process, we will be learning.

00:05:32.234 --> 00:05:35.174
And there's all kinds of room
there for people to make their own

00:05:35.174 --> 00:05:40.994
choices as to which activities they
prefer to do, which activities,

00:05:40.994 --> 00:05:43.094
keep them active with the language.

00:05:43.125 --> 00:05:45.854
And as long as you do
that, you will be learning.

00:05:46.034 --> 00:05:51.914
Even though very often we don't have the
feeling that we're learning and uh, so you

00:05:51.914 --> 00:05:53.414
know, how do we know if we're learning?

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:58.170
Uh, we should focus instead on how do
we know if we're enjoying the process.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:02.460
And, uh, with that, I'll just leave
you with a couple of videos that

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:07.349
I've done over the past couple
of years on related subjects.

00:06:08.039 --> 00:06:08.969
Thank you for listening.

00:06:08.969 --> 00:06:09.570
Bye for now.

